Oracle fixtures: real calculations, reduced to what the basis/labels
check needs.

Each file is a molecule that a real quantum chemistry code has actually
run, in the format tests/symmetry/testOracle.C reads:

    <point group>
    <number of atoms>
    <symbol> <x> <y> <z>        one line per atom, Angstrom, in the
                                symmetry frame autosym puts them in
    <functions per atom>        one integer per atom
    <angular momentum per function>
    <orbital labels>            one per orbital, the code's own

WHY THESE ARE CHECKED IN.  The MOPAC cases in run_tests.py are
generated on the fly, which keeps them honest but limits them to a
semiempirical valence basis: four functions an atom, s and p only, and
no spherical harmonics.  A real basis set is a different exercise --
def2-SVP puts fourteen functions on carbon including five spherical d
-- and that is the path ECCE actually uses.

orca-methanol: ORCA 6, B3LYP/def2-SVP, methanol in Cs.  48 basis
functions spanning 33A' + 15A'', which is what ORCA itself reports for
its 48 orbitals.  Taken from a calculation in the development
dataserver; the geometry and the labels are exactly what ECCE reads
back from its own stored properties.
